    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I hear you on the transparency part, and I definitely don't want to get caught in a lie. But that’s the problem—if I tell them the truth (that we simply missed the target), the investigation won't just end; they'll dig even deeper into our moderation logic and probably find that our performance is actually regressing. It'll be a disaster for the whole department.\n\nI'm not asking to invent a lie out of thin air. I'm asking if there's a genuine, plausible system failure that could realistically cause this specific kind of data drift. If I look into the telemetry ingestion logs and find even a tiny, legitimate intermittent error in how the 'unverified' tags are being processed during peak traffic, can I use that as the primary explanation for the discrepancy? \n\nI need to know if framing this as a \"telemetry/ingestion error\" is a technically defensible way to account for the gap between our internal module success and the external logs, without it looking like a desperate cover-up. Is there a technical reality where a silent error in the ingestion pipeline could actually cause the exact divergence the auditors are seeing right now?",
